i='TCS-3134';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1 (distributed output wording), Figure 1 (fixed k and suppressed polylogarithmic factors), Section 4.1 and Theorem 16 (multicolored reduction), Section 7, p. 15:15 (open independent-set question).'
refs.extend([dict(id='full',title='Beyond Distributed Subgraph Detection: Induced Subgraphs, Multicolored Problems and Graph Parameters — full version',authors='Janne H. Korhonen; Amir Nikabadi',year=2021,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06561',locator='Version 3, 7 December 2021: introduction repeats the output wording; Section 7 retains the question; Appendix A proves a detection statement by ensuring that at least one node reports.'),dict(id='paths2025',title='Distributed Complexity of P_k-Freeness: Decision and Certification',authors='Masayuki Miyamoto',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ISAAC.2025.51',locator='Introduction: explicitly defines ordinary detection by at least one yes-output, and distinguishes listing; results concern induced paths, not fixed-cardinality independent-set detection.')])
issue='Confirm the intended distributed output contract: Section 1.1 says that any node belonging to an admissible copy should report yes, whereas ordinary detection and the full paper’s Appendix A use an at-least-one-reporter criterion. These are different from deciding at every node whether it belongs to a k-independent set. Also record whether the communication network is promised connected and whether the requested optimal complexity is deterministic, randomized, or both; these choices are not fixed in the isolated question.'
save(i,dict(title='Round complexity of detecting k pairwise nonadjacent vertices in CONGEST',formal='For each fixed integer k≥2, determine the asymptotic number of CONGEST communication rounds needed to detect whether an n-vertex network graph contains k pairwise nonadjacent vertices. The source compares this with k-clique detection and treats k as constant when suppressing polylogarithmic factors in n. The target is a size-k independent set, not a maximal independent set or a set whose vertices have distance at least k. The precise distributed output, network-connectivity and randomness conventions must be confirmed before certifying matching bounds.',definitions='A simple undirected graph G=(V,E) has an independent set I⊆V of size k if |I|=k and no unordered pair of distinct vertices of I belongs to E. Let α(G) be the largest independent-set size; the underlying global predicate is α(G)≥k. In CONGEST, each graph vertex is a processor and the graph’s edges are the communication links. Computation proceeds in synchronous rounds; in one round a node can send a possibly different O(log n)-bit message across each incident edge in each direction and perform unrestricted local computation. The usual setting uses distinct O(log n)-bit node identifiers; knowing neighbors’ identifiers can be arranged in one initial round. The pattern size k and, in a fully specified version, the needed size information are known to the processors. There are no communication links between nonadjacent vertices. Ordinary existential detection has all nodes output no on a no-instance and at least one node output yes on a yes-instance. Membership detection instead requires each node to decide whether it lies in a copy; all-node global decision requires every node to know the global answer. The original introduction does not unambiguously select among these contracts. For a randomized version, a complete definition must require the entire output contract to hold with a fixed success probability, such as 2/3, on each graph and identifier assignment, and specify whether the round bound is worst-case. Complexity in the source’s fixed-k convention concerns n→∞ with factors depending only on k suppressed; the task does not ask for a bound independent of k when k grows with n.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Once the output, connectivity and randomness conventions are fixed from the source, give a round upper bound and a matching lower bound, up to polylogarithmic factors in n, for each fixed k. Do not replace communication in G by communication in its complement, or infer a k-independent-set bound solely from a clique or multicolored variant without a model-preserving implication.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Nonedges carry the desired pattern but cannot carry messages',text='An independent set is a collection of pairwise missing edges. In a centralized algorithm, taking the complement turns it into a clique. In CONGEST the network also determines where processors can communicate, so taking the complement changes the computing machine as well as the input predicate.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The problem differs from standard maximal independent set',text='A maximal independent set cannot be extended by adding a vertex, but need not have maximum cardinality. Finding such a set does not decide whether some independent set has at least k vertices. Here k specifies cardinality rather than a distance constraint.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The multicolored implication has a direction',text='The source shows that a T(n)-round algorithm for multicolored k-independent set yields an O(k²T(kn))-round algorithm for the uncolored problem. Its discussion explicitly declines to transfer every clique equivalence to independent sets in CONGEST.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Later induced-path bounds have a different target',text='The 2025 paper gives algorithms and lower bounds for induced paths and clearly states the at-least-one-reporter convention for detection. Those statements help distinguish output notions, but do not themselves determine the independent-set complexity requested here.',citation='paths2025'),dict(title='Remaining formulation issue',text=issue)],why='Would clarify a basic asymmetry between detecting edges and detecting nonedges when the input graph also limits communication, informing the distributed complexity of induced patterns.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='The source and its complete revised version leave fixed-k independent-set detection open and prove a directed reduction from the multicolored variant.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025',text='New induced-path detection results address related patterns in CONGEST, with an explicitly stated ordinary detection contract.',citation='paths2025')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the relevant complete conference and version-3 sections, including the contrasting introduction and Appendix A output formulations. Checked the 2025 induced-path paper and searched subsequent work through 12 September 2026; no matching independent-set classification was found. The output and model choices remain unfinished.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered fixed cardinality k, the CONGEST bandwidth model and the failure of graph complementation as a free reduction.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A basic distributed pattern problem whose relation to clique changes when missing edges cannot serve as communication links. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Replaced the truncated comparison with the independent-set predicate and fixed-k scope. Defined the network and distinguished ordinary detection, membership and all-node decision; kept unresolved source wording visible instead of certifying one invented contract.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The question is whether the communication graph contains k pairwise nonadjacent vertices.','Here k is a fixed cardinality parameter, not a distance or maximality requirement.','Nodes communicate only along existing edges using logarithmic-bit messages per round.','The centralized complement correspondence with clique is therefore unavailable for free.','The source’s distributed output and network/randomness conventions need clarification before its exact round complexity can be certified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','paths2025'])),notes=['Recovered the fixed-cardinality independent-set detection target and the fixed-k asymptotic convention.','Explained why complementation changes the communication model.','Checked full-version output wording and recorded the genuine detection-contract ambiguity.','Separated the multicolored implication and later induced-path results.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
